<Input><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal"><Font style="Text" bold="true">Problem 4 .</Font>  
<Font style="Text" bold="true">Definition</Font>. The <Font style="Text" italic="true">order</Font> <Font style="Text" italic="true">of an integer</Font> a <Font style="Text" italic="true">modulo an integer</Font> m is defined to be the least positive integer e such that <Equation executable="false" style="Normal" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.  

Write a procedure, call it <Font style="Text" bold="true">Ord</Font>, such that <Font style="Text" bold="true">Ord(a,m)</Font> returns <Font style="Text" bold="true">FAIL</Font> if the order does not exist and the order of <Font style="Text" bold="true">a modulo m</Font> if  it  exists.

<Font style="Text" italic="true">You may use the number theoretic result that the order of a modulo m exists if and only if </Font><Font style="Text" bold="true">igcd(a,m) = 1</Font><Font style="Text" italic="true">. Also you may use the fact that the order of </Font><Font style="Text" bold="true">a modulo m</Font><Font style="Text" italic="true"> is never greater than </Font><Font style="Text" bold="true">m - 1</Font><Font style="Text" italic="true">. [Note that Maple uses </Font><Font style="Text" bold="true">igcd</Font><Font style="Text" italic="true"> for the greatest common divisor of integers and </Font><Font style="Text" bold="true">gcd</Font><Font style="Text" italic="true"> for greatest common divisor of polynomials.] </Font></Text-field><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">
<Font bold="true">(a) </Font>Check that you obtain: <Font bold="true">

        Ord(0,11) = FAIL,    Ord(2,11) = 10,     Ord(4,11) = 5.</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
